            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The Sabbath symbolizes sacred rest, specifically the seventh day of cessation from labor, which foreshadows the believer's spiritual rest in Christ and the ultimate eschatological rest.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The Sabbath represents a complete unit of seven crowned by rest (such as the week itself or a seven-year cycle), not merely the day of rest.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The day of rest and the spiritual/eschatological rest it signifies.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A complete seven-part unit (the week itself) crowned by rest.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus limits the Sabbath to the day of rest (or the spiritual state of rest), whereas the book expands it to encompass the entire cycle of seven that the rest completes.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly denies that the Sabbath is solely the seventh-day rest itself, arguing instead that it refers to the complete unit of seven (the week) that the rest concludes.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book redefines the core referent of 'Sabbath' from the cessation of labor on the seventh day to the entire unit of seven (the week, the sabbatical cycle), citing linguistic evidence where 'sabbath' is translated as 'week'. This contradicts the consensus identification of the Sabbath strictly as the rest or the day of rest.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While 'sabbath' is used metonymically for the week in certain Greek and Hebrew idioms (because the week is bounded by Sabbaths), the root word fundamentally means 'cessation' or 'rest', and its primary theological focus in the biblical text is the rest itself rather than the six days preceding it.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
